    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "definition",
     "title": "Frequency density",
     "md": "In a histogram the **area** of each bar is proportional to the frequency. When the classes have unequal widths, the height of the bar is the **frequency density**: frequency density = frequency ÷ class width. The bars touch, there are no gaps, and the horizontal axis is a continuous scale."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "methode",
     "title": "Drawing and reading",
     "md": "Waiting times (minutes) at a clinic with frequencies 8, 14, 30, 12, 16 in the classes 0–10, 10–20, 20–40, 40–60, 60–100 have densities 0.8, 1.4, 1.5, 0.6, 0.4. To find the frequency in part of a class, multiply the density by the **width of that part**: between 50 and 60 minutes, 0.6 × 10 = 6 people."
    },
